  "presentation_summary": [
    "31-year-old woman presented after 10 days of nausea, poor appetite, back and joint pain, dark urine, and abdominal pain.",
    "She was initially evaluated as acute cholecystitis because of right-sided abdominal tenderness, painful right hypochondrial fullness, positive Murphy sign, and a palpable tender liver edge.",
    "Vital signs showed low-grade temperature 37.5 C, heart rate 92/min, and blood pressure 110/60 mmHg; scleral icterus was present.",
    "Initial labs showed leukopenia, anemia, mild thrombocytopenia, ALT 618 U/L, AST 559 U/L, ALP 371 U/L, GGT 147 U/L, total bilirubin 2.11 mg/dL, and direct bilirubin 1.92 mg/dL; prothrombin time, CRP, ESR, and urinalysis were reported normal.",
    "Ultrasound showed hydropic gallbladder without calculus, gallbladder wall thickness 14 mm, pericholecystic/perivesical fluid, and hepatosplenomegaly.",
    "HAV serology showed anti-HAV IgM and IgG positivity, while HBV, HCV, and Brucella testing were negative.",
    "Supportive care with IV fluids, antiemetic, acid suppression, and diet led to symptom regression within 4 days, gallbladder wall thickness regression to 3.5 mm, no surgery, and discharge on hospital day 8."
  ],
